 "result": "I don't recognize `tessel` as a standard tool, so I'd look in the repo for how it loads apps rather than guess.\n\n1. **The `tessel` CLI definition.** Check `pyproject.toml`/`setup.py` entry points, or a `tessel/cli.py`. This is where the app-loading logic lives. Look for how it imports your module and whether it calls a factory (`create_app()`, `make_app()`) with no arguments. Flask-style discovery does exactly that, so a required `config_name` makes it fail or skip the factory.\n2. **Its config or flags.** Check for a `tessel.toml`, `.flaskenv`, or an `--app`/`APP`/`TESSEL_APP` setting. These may let you point at `module:create_app(\"prod\")` or a pre-built object.\n3. **Your app module** (`__init__.py` or `app.py`). The simplest fix is to give `config_name` a default (e.g. `config_name=None`, falling back to an env var). Alternatively, add a small `wsgi.py` with `app = create_app(os.environ.get(\"APP_CONFIG\", \"default\"))` and point `tessel run` at it.\n\nI'd start with item 1, since it tells you exactly which call signature it expects.",
